+# Real-server validation: extended (V1, R-VAL-2)
+
+Template for a human to fill in against a real Plex server, the real plex.tv and, for the Pi rows, real Raspberry Pi hardware. Every parameter, flag and command the spec marked (verify) is listed. Only `Overall: PASS` closes the `real-server-validation-extended` phase (D11). A FAIL names the phase to reopen.
+
+**Secrets rule: never paste a token or a full `X-Plex-Token` URL into this file.** Check any pasted Diagnostics text or command output and delete any token or URL that carries one.
+
+Result values: PASS | FAIL (<phase to reopen>) | SKIPPED (<reason>, extended Pi rows only)
+
+How to fill in: replace the placeholder after each `Result:` with PASS or FAIL, write what you saw after `Notes:`, then replace the last line with `Overall: PASS` once every row is PASS (or SKIPPED where allowed). Only rows marked `skip if no Pi` may be SKIPPED, and the reason goes in Notes. Use `curl -H "X-Plex-Token: $(cat /dev/fd/3)" ... 3<tokenfile` style requests, never a token on the command line or in a URL, when you query Plex by hand.
+
+## A. First run and Plex parameters
+
+- [ ] V1-E01 — On a display with no saved settings and no `config.json`, open the app (first-run code built by `settings-sheet`) — expected: the first-run code appears large and upright on the empty-state card without any click (R-FIRST-1); approving it at plex.tv/link signs the display in; if it does not, reopen `settings-sheet` — Result: PENDING — Notes:
+- [ ] V1-E02 — Let the first-run code expire unapproved (code refresh built by `settings-sheet`; FAIL reopens it) — expected: the card refreshes to a new code by itself — Result: PENDING — Notes:
+- [ ] V1-E03 — Request a library with the contentRating filter by hand (parameter name and multi-value syntax as the app sends them; copy it from DevTools > Network) — expected: the server returns only items whose rating is in the list, so the parameter name and the separator are correct; if not, reopen `content-rating-filter` — Result: PENDING — Notes:
+- [ ] V1-E04 — List the distinct contentRating strings in your library (for example from `/library/sections/{key}/all` in DevTools or with the header-based request above). Record the country-prefixed ones in Notes, such as `gb/PG`, `gb/12A`, `de/12` — expected: every country-prefixed value that occurs matches the Q19 table (`gb/`, `de/`, `au/`, `ca/`, `fr/`, `nl/`); any value outside the table is listed in Notes and reopens `content-rating-filter` — Result: PENDING — Notes:
+- [ ] V1-E05 — Set the household limit to PG-13 (built by `content-rating-filter`; FAIL reopens it) and watch 20 poster changes on a library containing R-rated and gb/18 titles — expected: no R, NC-17, unrated or `gb/18` poster appears; a library of only R titles shows the "No posters found" message with a hint to raise the limit — Result: PENDING — Notes:
+- [ ] V1-E06 — Request `/library/sections/{key}/all?X-Plex-Container-Start=0&X-Plex-Container-Size=0` (with the token as a header) — expected: the response carries a totalSize attribute equal to the library's title count; the Settings library labels (built by `settings-sheet`; FAIL reopens it) read "Title · N titles" with the same number — Result: PENDING — Notes:
+- [ ] V1-E07 — Request a pool with `X-Plex-Container-Size` of 150 and of 500 (the oversampling cap, built by `content-rating-filter`; FAIL reopens it) — expected: both return without error and the larger one returns more items; a larger value is not rejected — Result: PENDING — Notes:
+- [ ] V1-E08 — Play a movie and read `/status/sessions` while it runs — expected: the entry has viewOffset and duration (milliseconds) and a Player.title naming the client; the progress bar (built by `metadata-and-now-playing`; FAIL reopens it) tracks it, jumping at most at the poll that follows a timeline report — Result: PENDING — Notes:
+- [ ] V1-E09 — Repeat the Sign in with Plex flow with DevTools > Network open and check the three plex.tv requests it makes over https: `POST /api/v2/pins`, `GET /api/v2/pins/{id}` and `GET /api/v2/resources` (do not copy request URLs or headers) — expected: all three answer with status 200 or 201, no CORS error and no redirect to http — Result: PENDING — Notes:
+
+## B. Token rotation
+
+- [ ] V1-E10 — Record which rotation route revoked the old token: removing the device under plex.tv > Account > Authorized Devices, or changing the password with Sign out of connected devices ticked. Test each route on a throwaway token if you can — expected: the route that works is named in Notes, and plex.tv `GET /api/v2/user` with the old token (sent as an `X-Plex-Token` header) returns HTTP 401; also note in Notes whether the account is a Plex Home user and whether the old token was server-specific, since the route may differ — Result: PENDING — Notes:
+
+## C. Chromium and Pi OS (skip if no Pi)
+
+- [ ] V1-E11 — Run `chromium-browser --version` (Bookworm) and `chromium --version` (Trixie); note which package name exists on each OS release (skip if no Pi) — expected: one of the two names runs on each OS release; record the name per OS in Notes (the README text arrives in the `docs` phase, which consumes this record) — Result: PENDING — Notes:
+- [ ] V1-E12 — Start Chromium with `--kiosk --app=<url> --overscroll-history-navigation=0 --disable-pinch`, then swipe back from the screen edge and pinch (skip if no Pi) — expected: the flag names are accepted, neither gesture leaves the app; if swipe-back still works, try `--disable-features=OverscrollHistoryNavigation` and note the outcome (overscroll-behavior: none is the backstop) — Result: PENDING — Notes:
+- [ ] V1-E13 — Set the Chromium policy `DeveloperToolsAvailability` to `2`, restart, and press F12 and Ctrl+Shift+I on a USB keyboard (skip if no Pi) — expected: DevTools do not open; `chrome://policy` lists the policy with value 2 — Result: PENDING — Notes:
+- [ ] V1-E14 — Check autostart on a Wayland (labwc) install via the labwc `autostart` file, and on an X11 install via XDG autostart (skip if no Pi) — expected: the kiosk starts after reboot under the method that matches the session type — Result: PENDING — Notes:
+- [ ] V1-E15 — Start Chromium with `--disk-cache-dir=/dev/shm/chromium-cache` and a `--disk-cache-size`, load posters, and list `/dev/shm/chromium-cache` (skip if no Pi) — expected: the directory fills and nothing new is written to the SD card's profile cache — Result: PENDING — Notes:
+- [ ] V1-E16 — With the app full-screen under labwc, leave a poster showing and check the Wake Lock (Diagnostics shows `Wake lock: held`) over 30 min, with OS blanking off (skip if no Pi; sleep behaviour built by `sleep-mode`, FAIL reopens it) — expected: the screen does not blank and `Wake lock: held` stays; a scheduled sleep shows pure black and playback still wakes it — Result: PENDING — Notes:
+- [ ] V1-E17 — Check the individual `translate` property used by pixel shift in the installed Chromium: in the console run `CSS.supports('translate','1px 1px')` (skip if no Pi) — expected: returns true, and pixel shift (built by `pixel-shift`; FAIL reopens it) visibly moves the stage — Result: PENDING — Notes:
+
+## D. Device helper (skip if no Pi)
+
+- [ ] V1-E18 — Run `cec-ctl -d /dev/cec0 --to 0 --standby`, then `cec-ctl -d /dev/cec0 --to 0 --image-view-on`, as a user in the `video` group (skip if no Pi) — expected: the TV goes to standby and wakes; the device opens without a permission error. Precondition: `hdmi_force_hotplug=1` is set in the boot config so the output stays enumerated while the TV is off; optionally also note in Notes the error seen when run by a user outside the group — Result: PENDING — Notes:
+- [ ] V1-E19 — Run `echo 'standby 0' | cec-client -s -d 1`, then `echo 'on 0' | cec-client -s -d 1` (skip if no Pi) — expected: the TV goes to standby and wakes; note the output name or adapter line in Notes — Result: PENDING — Notes:
+- [ ] V1-E20 — From a `systemctl --user` service, run the helper with `--helper` (flags built by `device-helper-server`; FAIL reopens it), and run `wlr-randr` to find the output name (for example HDMI-A-1), then `wlr-randr --output <name> --off` and `--on`. Confirm WAYLAND_DISPLAY and XDG_RUNTIME_DIR are passed (from the helper environment or `--helper-wayland-display` / `--helper-xdg-runtime-dir`) (skip if no Pi) — expected: without the two variables wlr-randr fails to connect; with them the output turns off and on — Result: PENDING — Notes:
+- [ ] V1-E21 — Read CPU temperature: `cat /sys/class/thermal/thermal_zone0/temp` and `curl -s localhost:8080/__helper/status` with the `X-Poster-Helper: 1` header (skip if no Pi) — expected: the file holds a number in millidegrees, `tempC` in the status matches it divided by 1000, and Diagnostics shows CPU temperature (built by `device-helper-integration`; FAIL reopens it) — Result: PENDING — Notes:
+- [ ] V1-E22 — With deviceHelper on, enter scheduled sleep so the screen powers off, then wake it by playing a title or at the schedule end — a power cycle test (skip if no Pi) — expected: after the power cycle the window is still full-screen, the poster is visible and playback wakes the display within the bounded time (the TV-wake delay is a named constant in the helper code: note the seconds you measured and the constant's name in Notes); if the page is hidden or not full-screen, reopen `device-helper-integration` — Result: PENDING — Notes:
+
+## E. Token handling
+
+- [ ] V1-E23 — In Settings with a PIN set, press Show on the token field (PIN gating built by `kiosk-and-pin`; FAIL reopens it), then close the sheet — expected: Show asks for the PIN, a wrong PIN leaves the field masked, the right PIN reveals it, closing the sheet masks it again; an exported settings file does not contain the PIN hash or salt — Result: PENDING — Notes:
+
+Overall: PENDING
